    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Earlier in this volume, Matthai (F-section, this volume) describes signatures in transient well tests\r\nof normal faults with a complex, heterogeneous permeability structure. This poster description\r\nsummarizes additional effects of small-scale heterogeneities in sandstone reservoirs on patterns\r\nof fluid flow and on fault signatures in transient well tests. Fluid flow is simulated in high-resolution finite-element models incorporating field measurements of permeability and structure of the Entrada sandstone, Arches National Park, Utah (Antonellini andAydin 1994, 1995).\r\n\r\nThe simulations show, for single-phase fluid flow and ignoring capillary effects, that less than\r\n10 cm wide zones of deformation bands have a negligible effect on radial drawdown from a well. Networks of open joints, however, strongly distort the radial drawdown pattern. They obliterate the characteristic response of faults in plots of fluid pressure in the drawdown tests, if the heterogeneitites occur between the well and the fault. Also, with regard to transient fluid flow in the reservoir, reservoir permeability is inadequately described by a single effective parameter in the presence of joints.\r\n\r\nOn the km-scale, simulations for overlapping normal faults identify that relay structures ctm strongly compartmentalize fluid pressure and asymmetric fluid pressure patterns may develop during their production.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
